# Russian → IPA transliteration rules (ru_ipa.rules)
#
# Source-Authors: Yanushevskaya, I. & Bunčić, D.
# Source-Year: 2015
# Source-Title: Russian
# Source-Container: Journal of the International Phonetic Association 45(2): 221-228
# Source-Id: https://doi.org/10.1017/S0025100314000395
#
# Phonemic, in the St Petersburg School framework the Illustration writes its
# broad transcriptions in (p. 221). Russian is in this set as the contact
# language the Turkic corpora borrow from, not as a Turkic language, so a
# word here is a citation form and the file is grapheme-driven like its
# siblings.
#
# Corroborated by the variety-matched description: Timberlake, A. (2004)
# A Reference Grammar of Russian, Cambridge University Press
# (ISBN 978-0-521-77292-1). Its mutable consonants -- those that can be
# either palatalised or not -- are ‹б в г д з к л м н п р с т ф х› (p. 18),
# which is exactly the $Cpair set below. The immutable ones are ‹ч щ›,
# intrinsically palatalised, and ‹ш ж ц›, intrinsically not (p. 18), which
# is why those five take no ʲ from this file. And "[ɨ] occurs after a hard
# consonant only if the consonant is unpaired or the word is a borrowing"
# (p. 35), which is the и rule in section 4. Pinned in
# test_russian_timberlake_words.py.
#
# The two symbol choices the Illustration leaves open are settled against
# Hamann, S. (2004) 'Retroflex fricatives in Slavic languages', Journal of
# the International Phonetic Association 34(1): 53-67
# (https://doi.org/10.1017/S0025100304001604), the study the Illustration
# itself cites for the retroflex reading. Hamann argues Russian ‹ш ж› are
# phonetically retroflex /ʂ ʐ/ (p. 61), and Epitran writes them that way.
# This file keeps the Illustration's own phoneme chart, /ʃ ʒ/ (p. 222),
# because the file is phonemic and retroflexion is a fact about
# realisation. For ‹щ› Hamann is direct evidence for what is written here:
# palatalising a retroflex changes its major place of articulation, so the
# palatalised sound "is not a retroflex but a palatoalveolar", written
# [ʃː(ʲ)] with the palatalisation redundant (p. 64). That is ʃʲː, not the
# alveolo-palatal ɕː the Illustration offers as an alternative (p. 223).
#
# In scope, because the Illustration makes them contrastive (p. 222):
#   - the palatalised/non-palatalised opposition, written ʲ on the consonant
#   - ж ш ц as unpaired hard, ч щ as unpaired soft
#   - и as ɨ after the unpaired hard consonants (pp. 224-225)
#   - iotation of е ё ю я, and the two signs
#
# Out of scope, because orthography does not determine them without stress
# or lexical knowledge. Each is stated by the source at the page given:
#   - vowel reduction, and the /e o/ that cannot appear unstressed (p. 225)
#   - final devoicing and regressive voicing assimilation (p. 223)
#   - assimilation of palatalisation, and cluster simplification (p. 223)
#   - loanwords keeping a hard consonant before /e/ (p. 223), which is
#     lexical: Гёте is /ˈɡʲote/, not the /ˈɡʲotʲe/ the orthography implies

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Macros (IPA, not Cyrillic)
#
# A left context is matched against text that has already been transliterated,
# so a rule looking leftwards names the IPA symbol rather than the Cyrillic
# letter. uzc_ipa.rules records the same constraint.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

$Cpair = [b v ɡ d z k l m n p r s t f x] ;
$Vout  = [a e i o u ɨ] ;

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. THE TWO SIGNS BEFORE A VOWEL
#
# Matched on the input, before either sign is rewritten, because ъ is deleted
# and its consonant would then look pairable and wrongly take ʲ. After ь the
# consonant is soft AND a /j/ is present: статья /statʲja/.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ъе > je ;   ЪЕ > je ;   ъЕ > je ;   ъе > je ;
Ъё > jo ;   ЪЁ > jo ;   ъЁ > jo ;   ъё > jo ;
Ъю > ju ;   ЪЮ > ju ;   ъЮ > ju ;   ъю > ju ;
Ъя > ja ;   ЪЯ > ja ;   ъЯ > ja ;   ъя > ja ;
Ъи > ji ;   ЪИ > ji ;   ъИ > ji ;   ъи > ji ;

Ье > ʲje ;  ЬЕ > ʲje ;  ьЕ > ʲje ;  ье > ʲje ;
Ьё > ʲjo ;  ЬЁ > ʲjo ;  ьЁ > ʲjo ;  ьё > ʲjo ;
Ью > ʲju ;  ЬЮ > ʲju ;  ьЮ > ʲju ;  ью > ʲju ;
Ья > ʲja ;  ЬЯ > ʲja ;  ьЯ > ʲja ;  ья > ʲja ;
Ьи > ʲji ;  ЬИ > ʲji ;  ьИ > ʲji ;  ьи > ʲji ;

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1b. ‹ц› BEFORE A VOWEL LETTER
#
# ц is unpaired hard, so it takes no ʲ and inserts no j. Written as input
# digraphs rather than as a left context on the vowel, because /t͡s/ is three
# characters wide and golden_sweep.py's probe coverage rests on every context
# being exactly one element wide. A digraph is a two-character source
# pattern, which that property already admits.
#
# These precede the bare ‹ц› rule below, since ICU takes the first rule that
# matches rather than the longest.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Це > t͡se ;  ЦЕ > t͡se ;  цЕ > t͡se ;  це > t͡se ;
Цё > t͡so ;  ЦЁ > t͡so ;  цЁ > t͡so ;  цё > t͡so ;
Цю > t͡su ;  ЦЮ > t͡su ;  цЮ > t͡su ;  цю > t͡su ;
Ця > t͡sa ;  ЦЯ > t͡sa ;  цЯ > t͡sa ;  ця > t͡sa ;
Ци > t͡sɨ ;  ЦИ > t͡sɨ ;  цИ > t͡sɨ ;  ци > t͡sɨ ;

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. CONSONANTS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Б > b ;  б > b ;
В > v ;  в > v ;
Г > ɡ ;  г > ɡ ;
Д > d ;  д > d ;
Ж > ʒ ;  ж > ʒ ;       # unpaired hard
З > z ;  з > z ;
Й > j ;  й > j ;
К > k ;  к > k ;
Л > l ;  л > l ;
М > m ;  м > m ;
Н > n ;  н > n ;
П > p ;  п > p ;
Р > r ;  р > r ;
С > s ;  с > s ;
Т > t ;  т > t ;
Ф > f ;  ф > f ;
Х > x ;  х > x ;

Ц > t͡s ;  ц > t͡s ;     # unpaired hard
Ч > t͡ʃʲ ; ч > t͡ʃʲ ;    # unpaired soft
Ш > ʃ ;  ш > ʃ ;       # unpaired hard
Щ > ʃʲː ; щ > ʃʲː ;    # unpaired soft, long

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. IOTATED VOWELS
#
# ц was taken above, as a digraph, because /t͡s/ ends in the same s that
# $Cpair contains and the general rule would otherwise palatalise it and turn
# солнце into solnt͡sʲe. The other unpaired consonants end in ʒ, ʃ, ʲ or ː and
# fall through to the default on their own.
#
# The contextual rules precede the anchored one because ICU's rule-masking
# check does not model the ^ anchor: it reads an anchored rule as having no
# left context at all, so an earlier ^ rule looks general enough to hide
# every contextual rule for the same letter and the file will not compile.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

$Cpair { Е > ʲe ;  $Cpair { е > ʲe ;
$Cpair { Ё > ʲo ;  $Cpair { ё > ʲo ;
$Cpair { Ю > ʲu ;  $Cpair { ю > ʲu ;
$Cpair { Я > ʲa ;  $Cpair { я > ʲa ;

$Vout { Е > je ;  $Vout { е > je ;
$Vout { Ё > jo ;  $Vout { ё > jo ;
$Vout { Ю > ju ;  $Vout { ю > ju ;
$Vout { Я > ja ;  $Vout { я > ja ;

^ Е > je ;  ^ е > je ;
^ Ё > jo ;  ^ ё > jo ;
^ Ю > ju ;  ^ ю > ju ;
^ Я > ja ;  ^ я > ja ;

Е > e ;  е > e ;
Ё > o ;  ё > o ;
Ю > u ;  ю > u ;
Я > a ;  я > a ;

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. И
#
# ɨ after the unpaired hard consonants, ʲi after a pairable one. Only
# palatalised consonants and /j/ occur before [i] (p. 222).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

ʒ { И > ɨ ;  ʒ { и > ɨ ;
ʃ { И > ɨ ;  ʃ { и > ɨ ;

$Cpair { И > ʲi ;  $Cpair { и > ʲi ;

И > i ;  и > i ;

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. REMAINING VOWELS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

А > a ;  а > a ;
Э > e ;  э > e ;       # "hard" e: after hard consonants, word-initial
О > o ;  о > o ;
У > u ;  у > u ;
Ы > ɨ ;  ы > ɨ ;

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. SIGNS NOT BEFORE A VOWEL
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ь > ʲ ;  ь > ʲ ;
Ъ >  ;   ъ >  ;

:: NFC ;
